1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Intermediate code is the machine-independent form between the front end and the back end.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
M languages and N machines: one form turns M x N generators into M front ends plus N back ends.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The optimiser is written once on that form and reused by every compiler built on it.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
GATE 2014 Set 3 asked this purpose and keyed the optimiser-reuse answer.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Three-address code: at most one operator per instruction, and at most three addresses.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
x = y + z * w is two instructions, not one: the order of evaluation must be explicit.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Six forms cover this lesson: binary, unary, copy, indexed load, and the two jumps.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Nothing here names a register or an instruction set, which is what machine-independent means.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Postorder walks the tree: left subtree, right subtree, then the operator.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Each operator emits exactly one instruction and one new temporary.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Leaves are source variables, so they cost no temporary at all.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Three operators give three instructions: t1 = a+b, t2 = c+d, t3 = t1*t2.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The number of temporaries used and the number that must exist at once are different questions.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
(a+b)*(c+d) uses three names but never needs more than two live at once.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The page finds the minimum by trying every legal evaluation order, not by a formula.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The register number counts leaves too, so a*b+c*d+e*f is 2 temporaries and 3 registers.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
In static single assignment form every definition gets a name of its own.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
So the count is the number of operators: eight for this expression.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
GATE 2015 Set 1 keyed 8 for exactly this expression.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The page generates both forms and checks that each computes the same values.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
An indexed load hides an address calculation.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
x = a[i] is two instructions: t1 = i*4, then t2 = a[t1].

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The scale is the element size, so an array of 24-byte records gives i*24.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
A call emits one param per argument, then the call, then a copy of the result.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A loop becomes labels and jumps, with the test at the top.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The while loop is five instructions, three labels and three jumps.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Every jump target must be a label that exists, and the page checks all of them.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The page executes the code for 25 input pairs and matches max(x, n) every time.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
a < b && c < d means: do not evaluate c < d unless a < b is true.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
In control context that is 7 instructions, and the second test runs in 4 cases out of 16.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
In value context the truth values are built first: 14 instructions, and both tests always run.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
If c < d could divide by zero, only the control translation is safe.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The same three instructions can be stored three ways.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Quadruples name the result; triples use the row number as the result.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Indirect triples add a table of pointers so a pass can move or delete a row.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Inserting a row breaks every triple reference after it; that is what the pointer table fixes.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Read the wording first: SSA, plain three-address code, or registers.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Then count operators for instructions, and definitions for SSA names.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
GATE 2016 Set 1 asked for total SSA variables: 5 inputs plus 5 definitions, so 10.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Two checks remove most wrong options before any arithmetic.

